-- Binding-layer satisfaction for anthill.geometry (proposal 038).
--
-- ── `fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]` IS WITHDRAWN (WI-931) ───────────────────
--
-- It stood here for the right reason — `VectorSpace requires Ring[F]`, and
-- `Float` being a `Ring` is a per-language binding fact, so a stdlib-only load
-- could not satisfy it — but it was never BACKED. `VectorSpace` declares four
-- functional members (`vec_add(a, b) -> V`, …) and `anthill.geometry` implements
-- the vector operations RELATIONALLY (`vec_add(?a, ?b, ?c)`), which WI-818
-- settled is specification the SLD world resolves against, not something the
-- evaluator can dispatch to. So the provision certified four operations that die
-- at the call.
--
-- It went unreported until now only because `Vec3` is a §6.3 free-standing
-- entity, and those emitted no `SortInfo` — so `check_provider_operations` read
-- the carrier as abstract and skipped it (WI-928).
--
-- WHY NOT SIMPLY ADD THE BODIES, which is what the ticket first tried: writing
-- `sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(…); operation vec_add(a, b) -> Vec3 = … }` introduces
-- a SECOND `vec_add` in `anthill.geometry`, and the short name silently moves to
-- it. MEASURED on a rule body doing `import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}` and
-- calling the relational form: ONE solution before, ZERO after, with no
-- diagnostic. Restoring the provision therefore needs the naming settled first —
-- WI-935, which owns both halves.
namespace anthill.geometry
  import anthill.prelude.{Float}
end
